    "cover": "images/cover-art-01.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-01.svg",
    "synopsis": "When Mira returns to her snowy hometown, she finds Noah trying to save the little bookshop they once loved.",
    "chapter": "The First Snow",
    "story": "Mira Santos had promised herself she would spend only one weekend in Bellweather. She had come home to help her aunt pack the old bookshop, not to remember the boy who used to leave paper stars between the pages of her favorite novels. But the first snow of December began falling just as she unlocked the shop, and there was Noah Reyes on the other side of the door, carrying a box of books and wearing the same crooked smile she remembered.\n\n“Your aunt said you might need help,” Noah said. “The roof leaks when it snows, and the town council is deciding whether this building can stay open.”\n\nMira wanted to tell him that the shop was only a building. Instead, she looked at the shelves where they had spent afternoons studying, arguing about endings, and planning lives much bigger than their small town. Her aunt had decided to retire, and without enough money for repairs, the shop would be sold at the end of the month. Noah had been keeping it running after work, but he could not save it alone.\n\nThey made a plan. Mira would use her design skills to organize a weekend book fair, while Noah contacted local writers and asked neighbors to donate books. For three evenings they worked side by side, hanging lights, fixing shelves, and sorting stories into boxes. At first, their conversation stayed safely practical. Then, while wrapping a stack of old paperbacks, Mira found a paper star with a message written in Noah’s handwriting: “For the person who always believes the next page can be better.”\n\n“You kept these?” she asked.\n\n“I kept a lot of things I thought I had to outgrow,” Noah admitted. “Including the hope that you’d come back.”\n\nMira told him why she had left: she had been afraid that staying meant giving up her dreams. Noah did not ask her to stay. He only said that a dream could change shape without becoming smaller. His words made her realize she had treated home like the opposite of ambition, when it might be a place from which she could begin again.\n\nOn the day of the fair, snow covered the street and people crowded into the shop. Children read by the window while musicians played outside. The town council members came to see the crowd and agreed to help fund the repairs if the community continued supporting the shop. Mira’s aunt cried when she saw the shelves full of people again.\n\nAfter the last customer left, Mira and Noah stood beneath the little paper stars they had hung from the ceiling. She had to return to the city on Monday, but she no longer felt that leaving and loving Bellweather were opposite choices. She could work remotely, visit often, and help the shop build an online store.\n\n“Does that mean you’re staying?” Noah asked.\n\n“It means I’m choosing what happens next,” Mira replied, taking his hand. “And I’d like you to be part of the next chapter.”\n\nOutside, the snow softened every sound. Inside the bookshop, Noah placed one last paper star between the pages of Mira’s favorite novel. This time, she did not leave it behind.",
