    "id": 2,
    "title": "The Lost Kingdom",
    "author": "Jamie Rose",
    "genre": "Fantasy",
    "cover": "images/cover-art-02.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-02.svg",
    "synopsis": "An apprentice cartographer follows an impossible map to a kingdom erased from history—and discovers why it disappeared.",
    "chapter": "Beyond the Mist",
    "story": "Lio was the youngest apprentice in the Royal Map House, where every road, river, and mountain had to be recorded before it could be trusted. He was good at drawing places he had seen, but his master often reminded him that a map could show where a person stood without explaining what a place meant. Lio thought this was a poetic way of saying he needed to work faster.\n\nOne rainy evening, he found an old brass compass hidden inside a damaged atlas. Its needle did not point north. It pointed toward a blank patch in the western mountains, a region every official map marked as empty. When Lio opened the atlas under candlelight, faint blue lines appeared, forming a path through the mist.\n\nHis master, Mara, recognized the symbol on the compass and went pale. “That is the mark of Avarra,” she said. “A kingdom removed from our records after its rulers tried to control the weather.”\n\nLio asked why a kingdom’s mistake should erase its people from history. Mara had no answer she was willing to give. Before dawn, Lio packed food, paper, and the compass, then left a note promising to return with proof. Mara caught up with him at the mountain gate, carrying a second lantern. “If you are going to ask dangerous questions,” she said, “at least take someone who knows how to read old maps.”\n\nThe trail led them through a forest where the trees grew in circles and the fog repeated their own voices. At the edge of a ravine, the compass stopped moving. Across the gap, they could see towers suspended between cliffs, connected by bridges of pale stone. Avarra was not empty. It had been hidden.\n\nInside the city, they met Neri, a young keeper of the weather bells. She explained that Avarra’s rulers had once built a machine to command storms. When the machine broke, the king tried to save the palace instead of the villages. The people shut the machine down, but the resulting mist surrounded the kingdom and made outsiders forget the roads leading to it. The old maps had been changed to protect Avarra from another ruler who might try the same thing.\n\nThe weather bells were failing, and Neri needed a map of the underground channels to repair them. Lio realized the compass was not leading him to treasure; it was leading him to a responsibility. He and Mara mapped the channels while Neri climbed the tower to ring the bells in the correct order. When the final bell sounded, the mist lifted, revealing farms, bridges, and a river that had been hidden for generations.\n\nLio could have returned to the capital as the discoverer of a lost kingdom. Instead, he asked the people of Avarra whether they wanted to be recorded. They agreed, on the condition that their history include both the rulers’ failure and the villagers’ courage. Lio drew the new map with a note beneath the city’s name: “A place is not empty simply because someone has chosen not to see it.”\n\nWhen he returned to the Map House, his master studied the map in silence. Then Mara placed the brass compass beside it. For the first time in centuries, the needle pointed north.",
