    "id": 3,
    "title": "The Secret Room",
    "author": "Taylor Gray",
    "genre": "Mystery",
    "cover": "images/cover-art-03.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-03.svg",
    "synopsis": "A student investigating strange noises in an old school discovers a hidden classroom and a forgotten promise.",
    "chapter": "Room 17B",
    "story": "Every afternoon at exactly 4:17, a bell rang from the locked east wing of San Isidro High. The school had no class there, and the wing had been closed since a ceiling leak years earlier. Most students ignored the sound. Bea Ramos, who stayed late for journalism club, wrote it down in her notebook three days in a row.\n\nHer friend Anton thought she was looking for a mystery because she was bored. Bea disagreed. The bell was followed by the sound of a chair scraping across the floor, and once she heard someone say, “Not yet.” When they asked the guard, Mang Rudy, he told them the east wing was empty. But he looked at the floor when he said it.\n\nThe school’s old yearbooks gave them their first clue. A photograph from twenty years earlier showed a classroom labeled 17B, with a teacher named Ms. Elena Cruz and a group of students holding a handmade sign: “No one studies alone.” The next yearbook had no class 17B, and Ms. Cruz’s name vanished from the staff list. Bea found a newspaper clipping about a flood that had damaged the wing and forced several students to transfer.\n\nThey asked Mang Rudy again, this time showing him the photograph. He sighed and unlocked the wing. Behind a warped noticeboard, they found a narrow door hidden beneath layers of paint. Inside was a small classroom filled with dusty desks, old books, and a metal box. The bell had been attached to a string that ran to the corridor; the scraping sound came from a loose shutter swinging in the wind.\n\nThe box held letters from former students. Many thanked Ms. Cruz for helping them catch up after illness, family problems, or long absences. One letter, written by a boy named Rafael, explained that the class had been a quiet place for students who were falling behind. After the flood, the room was closed and the program was never restarted. Ms. Cruz had left the school to care for her mother, but she had asked Mang Rudy to keep the letters safe until someone remembered the room.\n\nBea’s article could have made the discovery sound like a ghost story. Instead, she interviewed former students and learned how much the class had mattered. The principal admitted that the room had been forgotten during years of budget cuts. With the journalism club’s help, students cleaned it, repaired the window, and proposed a new after-school study space.\n\nAt the opening, Bea placed the old photograph beside a new one. Mang Rudy rang the recovered bell at 4:17, not to frighten anyone, but to invite students inside. Anton smiled at Bea. “So the mystery was a room?”\n\n“No,” she said, looking at the students helping one another with homework. “The mystery was how a whole school could forget something that important.”\n\nShe titled her article “Room 17B: The Place We Left Behind.” It was the first story she had written that changed something beyond the page.",
