    "id": 12,
    "title": "The Garden Under the City",
    "author": "Robin Park",
    "genre": "Eco-Fiction",
    "cover": "images/cover-art-12.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-12.svg",
    "synopsis": "Students discover an abandoned underground garden that could help their polluted city breathe again.",
    "chapter": "Green Beneath",
    "story": "The air in Greybridge had grown so dusty that the school advised students to keep their windows closed. Most people had accepted the gray sky as normal. Fourteen-year-old environmental club president Amara had not. She collected air-quality readings for a science project and noticed that the readings were slightly better near an old subway entrance that had been sealed for decades.\n\nWith her friends Dev and Luis, she followed the clue to a maintenance door beneath the station. Behind it lay a staircase covered in roots. At the bottom, they discovered a greenhouse built around a natural spring. Many plants had survived under the city, fed by moisture and light tubes powered by an old solar system. A notebook explained that the garden had once supplied fresh produce to the neighborhood during a period of heavy pollution.\n\nThe garden was failing. Its water filter had clogged, and the light system was losing power. Amara realized that the plants could help clean the air in the nearby school courtyard, but only if the group could repair the equipment and persuade the city to protect the space. Their teacher helped them contact a retired engineer who had worked on the greenhouse decades earlier.\n\nThe engineer showed them how to clean the filters and redirect water without damaging the spring. The students documented every step and invited local residents to help. Some adults dismissed the project as a school experiment, while others worried the garden would be turned into expensive apartments if the city learned about it. Amara understood their fear; the neighborhood had lost too many useful places to development.\n\nInstead of keeping the garden secret, the group presented their evidence at a public council meeting. They showed air readings, plant samples, and the greenhouse’s original plans. Amara spoke about how the garden had survived because people had cared for it, and how the city had become used to living with a problem it could no longer see clearly.\n\nThe council approved a community restoration project and protected the underground space from demolition. Volunteers repaired the light system, planted native species, and built a safe entrance for school visits. The garden did not solve all of Greybridge’s pollution, but it became a place where residents could learn, grow seedlings, and organize for cleaner streets.\n\nMonths later, Amara stood in the school courtyard as the first plants grown in the greenhouse were placed in new beds. The air was still imperfect, and the city still had work to do. But the students had proved that a forgotten solution could be brought back when people worked together.\n\nAmara wrote the final line of her science report: “The city did not need a miracle. It needed to stop ignoring the things already trying to help.”",
