    "id": 14,
    "title": "The Midnight Train",
    "author": "Drew Collins",
    "genre": "Thriller",
    "cover": "images/cover-art-14.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-14.svg",
    "synopsis": "A passenger aboard a train that stops at no stations must decode a message before sunrise to prevent a disaster.",
    "chapter": "Platform Zero",
    "story": "At 12:07 a.m., Mara boarded the last train home after missing the earlier service. The carriage was almost empty, and the route display showed a station she had never heard of: Platform Zero. When the train passed the usual stop without slowing down, she pressed the emergency call button. A recorded voice told her to remain calm and wait for the next announcement.\n\nA folded ticket lay on the seat opposite her. On its back was a series of numbers and the words, “If the train does not stop, find the person carrying the blue umbrella.” Mara looked around. At the far end of the carriage, an older man held a blue umbrella even though the night was dry. He saw her looking and moved to the next car.\n\nMara followed, keeping a safe distance. The man introduced himself as Mr. Delaine, a retired railway engineer. He explained that someone had taken control of the train’s routing system to force it toward an unfinished maintenance tunnel. The coded ticket had been left by a station worker who noticed the system was behaving strangely. If the train entered the tunnel at full speed, it would collide with a maintenance barrier.\n\nThe train’s intercom crackled, then went silent. Mara used the map on her phone to compare the train’s location with the numbers on the ticket. The sequence marked signal posts, not stations. She and Mr. Delaine moved through the cars, alerting the few passengers and asking them to stay seated while he explained how to reach the manual brake cabinet.\n\nAt the front carriage, they found the cabinet locked and the key missing. Mara noticed a fresh scratch around the emergency panel and realized the person who had altered the route had already been there. She found the key taped beneath the seat, exactly where a worker might hide it during a rushed repair. Mr. Delaine opened the cabinet and began slowing the train while Mara used the emergency radio to contact the control center.\n\nThe train shuddered as the lights flashed. For a frightening minute, the tunnel entrance filled the front window. Then the brakes caught, and the train stopped just before the barrier. Railway staff arrived through the service passage and safely escorted everyone out. The person responsible was identified through the station cameras and arrested without anyone being hurt.\n\nAt dawn, Mara finally reached home. She had boarded the train thinking of nothing more than getting to bed. Now she understood how small details—a ticket, a signal, a person who noticed a change—could matter when someone paid attention. She kept the coded ticket in her wallet, not as a trophy, but as a reminder to stay curious and ask for help.\n\nThe next week, the railway added clearer emergency instructions to every carriage. Mara wrote an article about the incident, focusing on the passengers who worked together instead of turning the night into a story about one hero. The title was simple: “The Train That Stopped Because People Listened.”",
