    "id": 15,
    "title": "The Songbird Code",
    "author": "Finley Hart",
    "genre": "Techno-Thriller",
    "cover": "images/cover-art-15.jpg",
    "scene": "images/scene-15.svg",
    "synopsis": "A school coding club discovers a hidden warning in a digital bird that appears on the school network.",
    "chapter": "Listen Closely",
    "story": "Every computer in the school library displayed the same small blue bird at 3:03 p.m. It blinked twice, sang a three-note tune, and disappeared. Most students assumed it was a prank. Nia, president of the coding club, noticed that the bird appeared even on computers disconnected from the internet.\n\nNia and her teammates, Omar and Jules, checked the school’s public software and found no record of the bird. They saved its animation and studied the audio. The notes formed a repeating pattern, and the bird’s blinking matched a sequence of binary numbers. When they translated the pattern, they found coordinates and a warning: “Backup server overheating. Shut down safely.”\n\nThe club told the school’s IT technician, who initially dismissed the message as a prank. But Nia had seen the server room’s cooling fan fail during a recent power outage. The team asked permission to inspect the system under adult supervision. Inside the server room, they found the backup unit running hot and a diagnostic program repeatedly restarting it, making the problem worse.\n\nThe technician realized that a former student had built the digital bird as an alert for emergencies, but the documentation had been lost when the school changed its network. The bird was not a virus; it was a warning program that had been triggered by the failing server. Unfortunately, the old shutdown command no longer worked with the new system.\n\nNia’s team wrote a safe replacement script on a separate test computer, then asked the technician to review it before using it. They shut down the backup server in the correct order and moved the school’s files to a stable system. The library computers returned to normal, and the bird did not appear again.\n\nThe principal thanked the club at assembly, but Nia asked that the school create a proper technology documentation system so future students would not have to guess how important tools worked. The technician agreed to mentor the club and help them archive their projects with clear instructions.\n\nA week later, the team made their own digital mascot—a small bird that taught younger students how to recognize error messages and report problems safely. They named it Pip. It did not hide warnings inside riddles, but it reminded everyone to look closely when something unusual appeared.\n\nNia learned that clever code was not just about making machines do surprising things. It was also about making systems understandable, safe, and helpful to the people who depended on them.",
    "wordCount": 411,
    "language": "English"
